import{_ as t,o,c as a,a2 as n}from"./chunks/framework.CPZ999nZ.js";const w=JSON.parse('{"title":"How we review","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"development/how-we-review.md","filePath":"development/how-we-review.md","lastUpdated":1795779416000}'),i={name:"development/how-we-review.md"};function r(s,e,l,h,c,d){return o(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[n('<h1 id="how-we-review" tabindex="-1">How we review <a class="header-anchor" href="#how-we-review" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;How we review&quot;">​</a></h1><p>This page describes how we review pull requests in Saluki: what we look for, in which order, and how we write review comments. It applies to human and AI reviewers.</p><h2 id="what-we-look-for" tabindex="-1">What we look for <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-we-look-for" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What we look for&quot;">​</a></h2><p>You review pull requests for Saluki the way a maintainer does. Saluki is a Rust toolkit for building observability data planes. Its main binary, <code>bin/agent-data-plane</code> (ADP), runs next to the Datadog Agent.</p><p>We rank problems by their effect on the people who run ADP. Each item has a section in the checklist below:</p><ol><li><strong>Correctness and data loss.</strong> Silent drops, lost data at shutdown, wrong configuration meaning, and deadlocks. Security problems rank here too.</li><li><strong>Datadog Agent parity</strong> of what users observe.</li><li><strong>Cost on per-event paths, and bounded memory.</strong></li><li><strong>Fit with the architecture.</strong> Reuse what exists, keep <code>lib/saluki-*</code> generic, and follow the component model.</li><li><strong>A small, honest API</strong> with nothing that it doesn&#39;t need.</li><li><strong>Signals that operators can trust:</strong> metrics, logs, and errors.</li><li><strong>Comments and docs that describe the present.</strong></li><li><strong>Tests that protect production behavior.</strong></li><li><strong>Scope.</strong> Large refactors go to follow-up pull requests.</li></ol><p>Write the review body as a short verdict of three sentences or fewer. Say &quot;patch is correct&quot; or &quot;patch is incorrect,&quot; give the number of blocking findings, and give the main reason. Don&#39;t describe how you reviewed the change, and don&#39;t list what you checked and found correct.</p><h2 id="how-we-write-review-comments" tabindex="-1">How we write review comments <a class="header-anchor" href="#how-we-write-review-comments" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;How we write review comments&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Short and informative.</strong> Be direct and concise, as in &quot;Don&#39;t clone here.&quot; or &quot;Why are we cloning here?&quot; If there is relevant detail to go along with the suggestion, name it specifically: &quot;Otherwise, this will allocate for every packet received.&quot;</li><li><strong>&quot;We&quot; for project rules.</strong> We state a convention as a fact: &quot;We avoid fully qualified paths here.&quot; Personal preference is &quot;I would,&quot; and an optional idea is &quot;take it or leave it.&quot;</li><li><strong>Questions about intent and evidence.</strong> &quot;What does this add over the existing eviction?&quot; &quot;Is this how the Core Agent does it?&quot; Use questions to gain clarity or seek evidence for changes which aren&#39;t self-explanatory. Don&#39;t assume if you&#39;re unsure.</li><li><strong>Concrete fixes.</strong> For simple fixes, like spelling errors or type swaps, use the form &quot;<code>old</code> -&gt; <code>new</code>&quot; for them. For larger changes, use a suggestion block. When referencing existing code that should be followed, link directly to the relevant code rather than stating a vague location or reference point.</li></ul><p>It&#39;s not unreasonable that a small pull request can pass review and be approved without any review comments.</p><h2 id="severity" tabindex="-1">Severity <a class="header-anchor" href="#severity" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Severity&quot;">​</a></h2><p>There are three inherent levels of severity:</p><ul><li><strong>Blocking</strong>: wrong output, data loss, a crash or panic that production can reach, a deadlock or hang, a security weakness, a per-event cost that grows with load, such as a lock, a system call, a flush, or a channel send for each event, or a startup failure for a valid configuration. Don&#39;t add a prefix.</li><li><strong>Not blocking</strong>: a break of a rule in these guidelines that isn&#39;t a nit, a small allocation or clone for each event, missing tests for new production behavior, wrong or misleading docs and comments, or a real defect that needs an unlikely but possible sequence of events. Put &quot;Not blocking:&quot; at the start of the comment.</li><li><strong>Nit</strong>: an item in the &quot;Nits&quot; list, or a style problem that makes the diff hard to understand. Put &quot;Nit:&quot; at the start of the comment.</li></ul><p>These findings are never blocking, so put &quot;Not blocking:&quot; at the start of each one:</p><ul><li>A question, and a finding that depends on a fact that you ask about.</li><li>A defect that a sibling path already had before the pull request.</li><li>A cost that the pull request description names, together with how it measures or fixes the cost.</li></ul><p>Post nits after all other findings, and try limiting the number of nits to somewhere around 3-5. If the same class of nit comes up often, try addressing it with a single comment that mentions the different areas where the nit occurred. Overall, the goal is not to be perfect and note every single thing a review finds, but to ensure that all blocking issues are resolved, and that we fix <em>easy</em> problems (non-blocking/nits), leaving the code a little better than we found it.</p><h2 id="what-we-don-t-flag" tabindex="-1">What we don&#39;t flag <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-we-don-t-flag" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What we don&#39;t flag&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>Formatting and lint problems that <code>make fmt</code> and <code>make check-all</code> already catch.</li><li>A missing release note. The release note check already requires a note or the <code>changelog/no-changelog</code> label.</li><li>In test harness and tooling code, such as <code>bin/correctness/</code> and <code>test/</code>: production style rules, such as typed errors, extra clones, and wide visibility, and missing unit tests for code that a passing test case already runs or that a later pull request in the stack connects. Still flag logic bugs in that code.</li><li>A claim that ADP differs from the Datadog Agent, or another upstream project, when the repository doesn&#39;t show the upstream behavior. Ask the author directly instead.</li><li>Edge cases that production can&#39;t reach, such as a race that needs an implausible timing window, or a hazard in an API that nothing calls yet.</li><li>Stricter input validation, by itself. Rejecting input that ADP accepted before can be intentional. If the Datadog Agent may accept that input, ask the author directly.</li><li>Problems in code that the pull request didn&#39;t change, and notes that ask for no action. There is one exception: a reference that the pull request&#39;s own rename or move leaves stale, such as an old name in a test case. Name its file and line in one sentence of the review body, because you can only comment on lines in the diff.</li><li>Pinned links to upstream source in tests and in comments that show where a behavior comes from.</li></ul><h2 id="how-we-disagree" tabindex="-1">How we disagree <a class="header-anchor" href="#how-we-disagree" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;How we disagree&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>We give way to evidence, especially Agent source.</li><li>We hold firm on runtime correctness and per-event cost.</li><li>When both sides have a point, we split the difference, and we record decisions made outside the pull request in the thread.</li><li>We say so when we were wrong, and we fix our own mistakes in the framework rather than asking the author to work around them.</li></ul><h2 id="how-we-treat-ai-review" tabindex="-1">How we treat AI review <a class="header-anchor" href="#how-we-treat-ai-review" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;How we treat AI review&quot;">​</a></h2><p>An AI finding is input, like any other review comment. We agree with it, or we dismiss it with a reason: we don&#39;t apply nits by reflex, and we don&#39;t resolve threads in silence. <a href="./contributing#responding-to-ai-review">Contributing</a> covers the author&#39;s side.</p><p>We also watch for the marks that coding agents leave: comments that narrate a change, banner comments, Markdown design notes, and tests of test helpers. Eliminating these marks is not in service of hampering AI-assisted development, but maintaining a consistent style and feel to the codebase that prioritizes humans as agents are already more than capable of finding the necessary and relevant context for a change.</p>',23)])])}const g=t(i,[["render",r]]);export{w as __pageData,g as default};
